{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":2,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516120963,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MTQyNjU1MTE4MQ=="},"title":"make master releasable by making it as usable as released version","was":"Toward 0.10 release of master"},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1516126128,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODA1MzA2Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358053066"},"message":"re 3: #2080 \n\nThe impression that anyone would need to get a \"deep understanding\" of anything is wrong. I could not replicate the behavior in those reports on anything other than the TB-size dataset on smaug. Small version of the dataset copied to other machines don't show this behavior. What needs doing is a definition of how we want whoosh to index the available metadata. That is for US to decide, and not for users. I am ready to enter this conversion at any time.\n\nMoreover, we need simplified structures to handle metadata of unforeseen structures (#2075 was brought up; #2034 is another flavor).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1516126236,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODA1MzY5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358053697"},"message":"re 2: I am absolute convinced that autodetection is a bad idea, infeasible from a resource-demand perspective, but at the same time absolutely doable. I will, however, not invest any time to make it happen, given my opinions ;-)\n\nIf you want to enable autodetection (or something that just behaves like it). It should already be possible to override `datalad.metadata.nativetype` with a list of all parser names. It will then try all parsers and take whatever they can find. I am not sure of our `ConfigManager` supports passing in a list of config values (if not, that would be its problem).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1516126365,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODA1NDQxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358054419"},"message":"re 1: Can we please discuss this in #2032 and not have an additional venue. Moreover, changing the default is as simple as changing the default to an existing config variable, see `datalad search --help` for the list of option to tweak the default behavior: `datalad.metadata.searchindex-documenttype = datasets`\n\nMoreover, one reason why index generation on the /// dataset takes so long is that we (not decided wether we)  value information about the existence of a file with a particular name. ATM there is an entry on each and every file. One could argue that there is nothing worth listing, if there is no metadata, as in the general case neither a meaningful/interpretable directory-structure nor filenames can be assumed. On /// this would remove the vast majority of all entries and speed up the process substantially.\n\nAnother angle would be to make Datalad multiprocess-safe and to enable multi-core index composition -- whoosh can do it, datalad cannot.\n\nAlso, it is likely the case that datalad queries git annex one more time when asking for aggregate metadata (as on smaug the datasets are actually locally available). This is F*CKING slow with many files. We could decide to not make it do that and should get a substantial performance gain -- at the cost of potentially outdated metadata.\n\nIn general, I am not convinced that we have a performance problem (the present implementation may well have one, but substantial changes are possible with minor code changes). But in order to figure that out we need to decide what price we are willing to pay for performance. Let's have that discussion.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1516126844,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODA1NjkyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358056924"},"message":"Given that the metadata implementation in master hasn't seen much attention over the past months, I also did not pay much attention myself. However, I already know that there is a series of issues that make changes to the structure of the metadata that I want to address for a number of additional use cases. Some of that work has already started, e.g. #2045. Some other stuff exists in local branches that I didn't manage to bring into a usable state yet. Those are motivated by concepts that we worked on over the past weeks. My primary source of motivation is not whether or not we release whatever branch, but the composition of a certain report that I will have to submit very soon ;-)\n\nIn any case, I much rather would like to have the long overdue discussion on what we want this system to do, and in which ways, and not how we can limit this new, more capable tool to do exactly what the old \"system\" did (and only did because it could do nothing else).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1516128919,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODA2NzE3Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358067172"},"message":"one more: the fact that we are in this situation is proof that we have trouble with the discrepancy of the desire for stability vs the similarly desire to break things in order to make progress without getting eaten up in backward compatibility duties (let's call that the Microsoft paradigm ;-) I want to express that I would have never sent a PR for the first `search` implementation, if I would have known that its presence would present such a high bar for future improvements.\n\nSome time back I looked at the connection points of the metadata support with the rest of the code base, and there are _very_ few. We should really consider throwing it out of the core code base. There could be a separate project, Datalad core could keep its original search functionality, and any dataset provider can decide which one to use. Having an external consumer of Datalad core API should also have a beneficial effect on its general utility over time.\n\nHeck, this could be a set of plugins...and it would be a good reason to elevate the plugin interface to behave like full-scale commands.... I start to like this... Maybe this could be a general path for additions/changes of datalad components. Implement them as plugins, bring them to people, let people decide what works and which way and only eventually move proven pieces into the core code base.\n\nedit by @yarikoptic: copy/pasted the 2 last paragraphs into https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2082","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1516131780,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODA4MTEzNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358081137"},"message":"\u003e If you agree that those are indeed the problems to address, let's move them into dedicated issues and resolve them.\n\nI agree. Those are things to discuss/address and we should split this into dedicated issues. Otherwise the discussion will be \"unusable\" itself. ;-)\n\nGenerally, I'd like to state, that it took me half a day to dive into whoosh and then I was able to provide fixes and extensions to Michael's PR back then, as well as I was able to perform some basic searches on my datasets. That clearly means, it's not about the \"deepness of understanding\". The troubles you experience have to have another reason. (Nevertheless, I'm absolutely fine with providing additional convenience, so for some basic things users don't need to lookup whoosh's language)\nSo, in conclusion, we should approach the discussion by first figuring out, what's the issues you apparently constantly have with the searches. Let's prepare a hangout to clarify and possibly reproduce what you are doing on other machines. Even better vice versa. We provide simple, fully defined examples and then let's see whether they work for you (on smaug and elsewhere). Starting with a single, simple dataset. Then raise complexity and narrow down, where exactly it stops working. We need to be on the same page regarding here before discussing anything else, since all those discussions are influenced by that feeling, that somehow nothing seems to work for you. Or - if that would take too long for a hangout session, let's have just another issue for that.\nAnd please split into several issues. :-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516137597,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODEwOTY3NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358109675"},"message":"@bpoldrack thanks for offering examples, they would be super helpful.  And indeed lets continue in related/linked issues (thus closing this one).","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516137597,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTQyNzA2MjAxMw=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516138893,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODExNjQ1OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-358116458"},"message":"Although, @bpoldrack , I think #2032 already provides some good examples. I think quite often it is again some bugs which ruin my \"experience\" (like today `datalad search 'bids:participant_id:sub-01' type:dataset ` didn't work in one attempt on the `///openfmri` clone, but worked when I recreated a minimalist example)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516739975,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1OTkyMjg5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-359922897"},"message":"\u003e Datalad core could keep its original search functionality\n\nI think that it indeed could be our way out of a release deadlock ATM:\n\n- what if master's `search` would become `search-whoosh`\n- we reincarnate simplistic `search` of 0.9.x and RF it to support new metadata ds* aggregated metadata instead of the old one. AFAIK new ds* level metadata structure is easy and old style search is very dumb so I do not expect major problems making it actually work and creating its \"search index\" in reasonable time.\n\nHaving two search commands will also be the demonstration of having multiple metadata \"consumers\" (echoing discussion in #2082 etc) without a sacrifice of dumb/simplistic/humane search interfaces which would not require extensive index generation.\n\nmeanwhile I could aggregate metadata within our datasets.datalad.org using both 0.9.x and master (tricky but should be doable if we add `--since` handling to aggregate-metadata in 0.9.x), or (easier on me ;)) we just leave 0.9.x style metadata inplace and ask ppl to upgrade to a new flashy 0.10.\n\nAs I have noted before, and reflecting on above use case, we should also add versioning marker to all metadata files, so in the future we could inform users that a new version of metadata detected and update is recommended.  We rely on the same major.minor.patch versioning to signal breakage.enhancements.fixes with demanding.recommending.ignoring levels of the announcement demanding the datalad upgrade.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516739976,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEzOlJlb3BlbmVkRXZlbnQxNDM4MTg3ODk3"},"status":1},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518255014,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NDYzODc0OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-364638748"},"message":"#2127 now provides old-style string matching search -- albeit still with whoosh (I see no point in a custom approach). It also builds a default search index for datasets only as before. @yarikoptic wanted to look into a metadata autodetection helper (#2084). The rest has been dealt with before.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1518277564,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NDY2NDIwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-364664200"},"message":"Great. I will it all a try later today tomorrow. Knowing what state of code to use to aggregate would help","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518277936,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NDY2NDY4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-364664684"},"message":"#2115 is pretty much \"master\" from my POV. I do not know what criteria would be applicable for a merge into master. The last change to the aggregation layout is much further back, but cannot easily pinpoint a commit, and there seems to be no need to try anything older.","files":null},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1518659393,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MTQ3NTA1MTEzMw=="},"title":"Toward 0.10 release of master","was":"make master releasable by making it as usable as released version"},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1521291241,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3MzkxNzk4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-373917986"},"message":"Just reread this. I cannot easily see any concrete tasks that I can perform to make 0.10 a reality (please tell me if that is wrong). Once we get green light for the metadata/aggregate/search combo, they need to get a final round of doc updates to reflect whatever behavior they will exhibit at that point.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1521468983,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3NDIyNjkxMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-374226913"},"message":"my main obstacle remains inability to use search any time I have tried: #2262, #2316 \nOverall: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+milestone%3A%22Release+0.10.0%22 -- I have added those two to 0.10.0 as well.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1521470162,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3NDIzMzkzMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-374233931"},"message":"Hmm, my point remains. No concrete tasks for me.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1521475751,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3NDI2ODEyOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2079#issuecomment-374268128"},"message":"ok, here are some concrete ones: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues?q=assignee%3Amih+is%3Aopen+milestone%3A%22Release+0.10.0%22","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524832187,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE1OTg2NjA1Njg="},"added":["conference agenda item"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524842752,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50MTU5OTAzMDU1MQ=="},"added":[],"removed":["conference agenda item"]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528780428,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5NjQ2Nzg3MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/